<h1>Video 013 — main() method, part 1</h1>
<h2>Video info</h2>
<p>Title: Core Java with OCJP/SCJP: Language Fundamentals Part-13 || main() method part-1</p>
<p>YouTube has captions disabled on this upload. Video 012 closes var-args with “next we perform postmortem on main method, almost two sessions, then command line arguments.” This is session 1 of that postmortem. Video 014 is session 2 (overload, inherit, static block vs main). Video 015 is command-line arguments.</p>
<h2>What this lecture covers</h2>
<ol><li>Why main exists (JVM entry point)</li></ol>
<ol><li>Word-by-word meaning of public static void main(String[] args)</li></ol>
<ol><li>What happens if you drop or change each word (NoSuchMethodError)</li></ol>
<ol><li>Legal rearrangements and extra modifiers</li></ol>
<ol><li>String[] args vs String args[] vs String... args</li></ol>
<ol><li>Changing the name args</li></ol>
<h2>Detailed notes</h2>
<h3>Who calls `main`?</h3>
<p>You never call main from your own code as the start of the program. You type:</p>
<p>java Test</p>
<p>The JVM loads Test, then looks for a method with a fixed protocol:</p>
<pre class='code'>public static void main(String[] args)</pre>
<p>If that exact contract is missing, you do not get a compiler error for “no main” in a normal class compile. You get a runtime error when you launch:</p>
<p>NoSuchMethodError: main</p>
<p>(On newer Java the message is often Error: Main method not found in class Test, please define the main method as: public static void main(String[] args).)</p>
<p>Durga’s point: main is a protocol between programmer and JVM, not a language keyword. main is an identifier. That is why Main is a different method.</p>
<h3>`public`</h3>
<p>JVM is outside the class (it is not your code). It must be allowed to call main from anywhere → public.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    private static void main(String[] args) { }   // launches: NoSuchMethodError
}</pre>
<p>Same story for default (no modifier) and protected. The JVM’s search is for public main.</p>
<h3>`static`</h3>
<p>JVM calls main without creating an object. There is no new Test() before main. So main must be static.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public void main(String[] args) { }  // NoSuchMethodError — not static
}</pre>
<p>If it were instance, JVM would have to guess a constructor and create an object — Java does not do that for startup.</p>
<h3>`void`</h3>
<p>JVM does not use a return value from main. Return type must be void.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static int main(String[] args) {
        return 0;   // NoSuchMethodError — not void
    }
}</pre>
<p>(C’s int main is a different language.)</p>
<h3>`main` — the name</h3>
<p>The name must be `main`, exact case.</p>
<pre class='code'>public static void Main(String[] args) { }  // NoSuchMethodError
public static void MAIN(String[] args) { }  // NoSuchMethodError</pre>
<p>Those compile as ordinary methods. JVM just will not call them as the entry point.</p>
<h3>`String[] args`</h3>
<p>Command-line arguments are strings (video 015). So the parameter is an array of String.</p>
<p>Legal forms Durga writes:</p>
<pre class='code'>public static void main(String[] args) { }
public static void main(String args[]) { }
public static void main(String []args) { }</pre>
<p>From Java 1.5, var-arg form is also an entry point (internally an array):</p>
<pre class='code'>public static void main(String... args) { }</pre>
<p>Not an entry point:</p>
<pre class='code'>public static void main(String args) { }     // one String, not array
public static void main() { }                // no args
public static void main(int[] args) { }      // wrong element type</pre>
<p>Those compile; JVM ignores them for launch.</p>
<h3>Parameter name is your identifier</h3>
<p>args is only a convention (video 016 coding standards: even args is the usual name). Any valid identifier works:</p>
<pre class='code'>public static void main(String[] a) { }
public static void main(String[] dhanush) { }
public static void main(String[] values) { }</pre>
<p>Still the entry point. Inside, use that name: dhanush.length, not args.length.</p>
<h3>Order of `public` and `static`</h3>
<p>Modifier order is not fixed:</p>
<p>static public void main(String[] args) { }   // valid entry point</p>
<p>void must stay the return type (before the name). You cannot write public static main void.</p>
<h3>Extra modifiers that are still an entry point</h3>
<p>You may add:</p>
<ul><li>final — cannot hide/override in the usual instance way; for static it is “cannot hide with same signature in child” in the sense of final static</li></ul>
<ul><li>synchronized — JVM still calls it; it just acquires the class lock</li></ul>
<ul><li>strictfp — IEEE floating-point strictness</li></ul>
<pre class='code'>public static final synchronized strictfp void main(String[] args) { }</pre>
<p>This is still a legal main for the JVM. Durga puts this on the board as a “don’t panic in the exam” example.</p>
<p>Not allowed as a working Java main body:</p>
<ul><li>abstract — no body; class would have to be abstract; JVM still would not treat it as a runnable entry with a body</li></ul>
<ul><li>native — no Java body</li></ul>
<p>Keep abstract / native off main for this lecture’s exam answers unless the question is specifically about compile vs launch.</p>
<h3>Compile vs run — Durga’s two-step check</h3>
<p>For each variant, ask:</p>
<ol><li>Does it compile? (Java language rules)</li></ol>
<ol><li>Can we launch with `java ClassName`? (JVM protocol)</li></ol>
<p>Examples:</p>
<p>The class can still have those methods. They are just not the JVM entry.</p>
<h3>Why this is “language fundamentals”</h3>
<p>OCJP loves mixing:</p>
<ul><li>a legal main that is not the protocol</li></ul>
<ul><li>two methods named main (overloading — video 014)</li></ul>
<ul><li>String[] vs String...</li></ul>
<p>Part 1 is: one method, tear it apart word by word.</p>
<h3>`String` is not a keyword</h3>
<p>Reminder from video 001: you could theoretically name a variable String, which would make String[] args a mess. Don’t. The parameter type must be java.lang.String.</p>
<h2>Code from the lecture</h2>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    static public final synchronized strictfp void main(String... dhanush) {
        System.out.println(&quot;entry point&quot;);
        System.out.println(dhanush.length);
    }

    public static void main(int[] args) {
        System.out.println(&quot;overloaded — JVM ignores this on launch&quot;);
    }
}</pre>
<p>Launch:</p>
<p>java Test</p>
<p>Prints entry point then 0. The int[] overload is unused.</p>
<h2>Rules to memorize</h2>
<ol><li>Protocol: public static void main(String[] args) (or String args[] or String... args).</li></ol>
<ol><li>Missing public / static / void / name main / String array-or-varargs → launch error, usually still compiles.</li></ol>
<ol><li>public and static may swap order.</li></ol>
<ol><li>final, synchronized, strictfp may be added.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Parameter name can be anything; type cannot.</li></ol>
<h2>Exam / interview traps</h2>
<ul><li>“CE because static is after public” — false.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Main vs main — compiles, does not launch.</li></ul>
<ul><li>String... args is a valid entry point (1.5+).</li></ul>
<ul><li>No compiler error for a class without main; failure is at run.</li></ul>
<ul><li>public static void main(String args) is not the protocol.</li></ul>
<h2>Recap</h2>
<p>Part 1 is the JVM contract. Part 2 is what happens when there are several mains, inheritance, and static blocks.</p>
<p>Next video: 014-main-method-part-2.md</p>
